        "original_id": "eae7df29-ae40-5974-8277-e505e2fd2fa1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Theodor W. Adorno, Donald Gramm\n- Works: Lulu, Culture Industry Reconsidered, Falstaff, Don Giovanni\n- Roles: Dr. Schön, Jack the Ripper, Leporello\n- Topics: critical theory, Frankfurt School\n\nFacts:\n- Theodor W. Adorno was born as Theodor Ludwig Wiesengrund on September 11, 1903, and he was a German philosopher, sociologist, and composer known for his critical theory of society.\n- In 1963, Theodor W. Adorno wrote Culture Industry Reconsidered, originally titled \"Résumé über Kulturindustrie,\" and he belonged to the Frankfurt School of social theory.\n- Donald Gramm performed notable operatic roles including the title role in Verdi's Falstaff, Leporello in Mozart's Don Giovanni, and Dr. Schön and Jack the Ripper in Berg's Lulu.\n- Theodor W. Adorno wrote that the opera Lulu is a work whose quality becomes more apparent the longer and more deeply one immerses oneself in it.\n- Theodor W. Adorno died on August 6, 1969.",
